Yes. The previous rate \(\varepsilon^{4/(3d+8)}\) is not the best consequence of the argument. A sharper use of Proposition 2.3 gives

\[
\boxed{
\|h_n^D-h\|_{L^\infty(V_n[U])}
\le C\|h\|_{C^2(K)}
\varepsilon_n^{\,4/(d+4)}.
}
\]

The key improvement is to estimate the expected exit time of a small-volume set rather than choosing a deterministic time.

## 1. Expected exit time of a set

Let \(S\) be an interior finite vertex set and write
\[
R:=\left(\sum_{v\in S}\operatorname{Vol}_d(P_v)\right)^{1/d}.
\]
Proposition 2.3 implies that the first Dirichlet eigenvalue on \(S\) satisfies
\[
\lambda_1(S)\ge \frac{c_d}{R^2+\varepsilon R}.
\tag{1}
\]

Set
\[
\rho:=R^2+\varepsilon R.
\]
Proposition 3.1 gives
\[
p_{2\rho}^S(x,x)
\le C_d\left(\rho^{-d/2}+\varepsilon^d\rho^{-d}\right).
\]
Since \(\operatorname{Vol}(S)=R^d\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Vol}(S)p_{2\rho}^S(x,x)
&\le C_d\left[
\left(\frac{R^2}{\rho}\right)^{d/2}
+
\left(\frac{\varepsilon R}{\rho}\right)^d
\right]\\
&\le C_d.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]

For \(t\ge \rho\), the \(L^2\) spectral contraction and (2) give
\[
\mathbb P_x(\tau_S>t)
\le C_d\exp\left(-\frac{c_d(t-\rho)}{\rho}\right).
\]
Integrating the survival probability,
\[
\boxed{
\sup_{x\in S}\mathbb E_x[\tau_S]
\le C_d(R^2+\varepsilon R).
}
\tag{3}
\]

This is the discrete analogue of the fact that Brownian motion exits a set of volume \(R^d\) in expected time at most \(CR^2\).

## 2. Apply this to a superlevel set

Fix \(\eta>0\) and set
\[
S_n:=\{v:h_n^D(v)-h(v)>\eta/2\}.
\]
The \(L^2\) estimate gives
\[
\sum_{v\in S_n}\operatorname{Vol}_d(P_v)
\le C\|h\|_{C^2(K)}^2\frac{\varepsilon_n^2}{\eta^2}.
\]
After normalizing \(\|h\|_{C^2(K)}\le1\), this means
\[
R_n\le C\left(\frac{\varepsilon_n}{\eta}\right)^{2/d}.
\tag{4}
\]

Fix a small radius \(r>0\), independent of \(n\), and stop the walk at
\[
\sigma=\tau_{S_n}\wedge\tau_{B_r(z)}.
\]
The coordinate functions are discrete harmonic, so
\[
\mathbb E_z[X_\sigma-z]=0.
\tag{5}
\]
The exact clock identity and (3) give
\[
\mathbb E_z\|X_\sigma-z\|^2
\le C\left(R_n^2+\varepsilon_nR_n+\varepsilon_n\right).
\tag{6}
\]

Taylor‚Äôs theorem, together with (5), gives
\[
\mathbb E_z[h(X_\sigma)-h(z)]
\le
C\|\nabla^2h\|_\infty
\mathbb E_z\|X_\sigma-z\|^2.
\tag{7}
\]
Furthermore,
\[
\mathbb P_z(\tau_{B_r(z)}<\tau_{S_n})
\le
\frac{1}{r^2}\mathbb E_z\|X_\sigma-z\|^2.
\tag{8}
\]

Optional stopping for \(h_n^D\), using (7)‚Äì(8), therefore yields
\[
\sup_{v\in V_n[U]}(h_n^D(v)-h(v))
\le
C\left(
\eta+R_n^2+\varepsilon_nR_n+\varepsilon_n
\right).
\]
Applying the same argument to \((-h_n^D,-h)\),
\[
\|h_n^D-h\|_\infty
\le
C\left[
\eta+
\left(\frac{\varepsilon_n}{\eta}\right)^{4/d}
+
\varepsilon_n
\left(\frac{\varepsilon_n}{\eta}\right)^{2/d}
+\varepsilon_n
\right].
\tag{9}
\]

## 3. Optimize

Balance the first two terms:
\[
\eta=
\left(\frac{\varepsilon_n}{\eta}\right)^{4/d}.
\]
This gives
\[
\eta=\varepsilon_n^{4/(d+4)}.
\]
The remaining terms in (9) are smaller. Hence
\[
\boxed{
\|h_n^D-h\|_\infty
\le
C_{U,K,d}\|h\|_{C^2(K)}
\varepsilon_n^{4/(d+4)}.
}
\]

For example,
\[
d=2:\ \varepsilon_n^{2/3},\qquad
d=3:\ \varepsilon_n^{4/7},\qquad
d=4:\ \varepsilon_n^{1/2}.
\]

This is likely the natural rate obtainable from the combination of an \(L^2\)-error of order \(\varepsilon\) and diffusive exit-time control.
endstream
